EEE383 — Dual-Sensor Hybrid Seismic Alert System

**Power System Analysis ****&**** Risk Log**

Current budget, overcurrent protection, and simulation-verified headroom

*Dual-Sensor Hybrid Seismic Alert System — EEE383, Group 01*

Revision 2 — includes Proteus-measured verification data

# Executive summary

Everything below is ranked by how much it actually needs your attention. Full detail for each row is in the referenced section.

| **Status** | **Item** | **Section** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **CRITICAL — OPEN** | LM2596 buck cannot regulate from a single-cell LiPo (needs ~4.5-5V in) — conflicts with the battery-only operating mode. | *§1, §7* |
| **OPEN DECISION** | Shared vs. separate polyfuse between the two boards — combined peak (642 mA) exceeds a single 500 mA fuse's hold current. | *§4.3* |
| **MONITOR** | AP2112K-3.3 dropout below ~3.55V battery — 3.3V rail will sag before the cell is truly empty. | *§5* |
| **MONITOR** | TP4056 protection circuitry assumes a battery is present on OUT+ — not meant as a bare USB pass-through. | *§7* |
| **UNVERIFIED** | RBD-2229 and passive buzzer current draw are estimates — no manufacturer figure exists for either. | *§2, §3* |
| **CONFIRMED OK** | Per-board polyfuse margin (26-45%), LDO headroom (61% of rating), and battery discharge rate (0.64C) are all comfortable. | *§4.2, §5* |
| **CONFIRMED OK** | Op-amp output headroom confirmed from real Proteus data — only 3% of the 0-3.3V window used, nowhere near clipping. | *§6* |
| **CONFIRMED OK** | Piezo signal current scales with vibration size but stays sub-microamp even at peak — confirmed against real simulation data. | *§6.2* |

**Read as: **CRITICAL needs a design decision before this can be called final. OPEN DECISION needs you to pick an option. MONITOR is a firmware/usage note, not a hardware defect. UNVERIFIED just needs a 2-minute bench measurement. CONFIRMED OK needs nothing further.

# 0. Scope and assumptions

- Two physical PCBs: Sensing PCB (ESP32-S3-WROOM-1, MPU-6050 breakout, RBD-2229 piezo module, LM358N conditioning stage) and Alarm PCB (ESP32-WROOM-32, common-anode RGB LED, passive piezo buzzer).

- Each board is assumed to carry its own AP2112K-3.3 (or equivalent) LDO and its own 500 mA polyfuse on BAT+, consistent with the existing 3.1 schematic pattern. §4.3 also covers what happens if that assumption is wrong.

The LM2596 buck converter's placement is the single biggest open question in this report — see the CRITICAL item above and §7. Everything else below assumes the LDO path, which is the only part of the power architecture that's actually confirmed.

# 1. Power architecture (current state)

The confirmed path (LiPo → TP4056 → polyfuse → LDO → loads) is shown in gray/blue. The LM2596 is shown in red because its role and connection point are not yet decided, and — as drawn — it cannot work the way a battery-powered buck normally would.

# 2. Sensing PCB current & power budget

All sensing-PCB loads run from the 3.3V rail produced by the AP2112K-3.3 LDO. Two conditions are evaluated: typical (Wi-Fi/ESP-NOW radio idle) and peak (an ESP-NOW transmit burst in progress).

| **Component** | **Condition** | **Current** | **Source** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ESP32-S3-WROOM-1 | Active, CPU running, RF idle | 23.88 mA (measured) | Espressif [2] |
| ESP32-S3-WROOM-1 | Wi-Fi/ESP-NOW RX | 95 – 100 mA | Espressif [1], Table 6-4 |
| ESP32-S3-WROOM-1 | Wi-Fi/ESP-NOW TX (peak, 802.11b worst case) | 355 mA | Espressif [1], Table 6-4 |
| MPU-6050 (bare IC) | Gyro + Accel + DMP active | 3.9 mA | InvenSense [3] |
| MPU-6050 breakout (GY-521 style) | Active (incl. onboard LDO + power LED) | ≈ 8 mA | CentIoT GY-521 spec [4] |
| LM358N | Quiescent, VCC ≈ 5V, full temp. | 0.5 mA typ / 1.2 mA max | UNISONIC LM358 [5] |
| RBD-2229 piezo module | Not published by manufacturer | ≈ 5 mA (assumed) | Unverified — see §8 |
| Pull-ups / decoupling | Static (4.7 kΩ I²C pull-ups etc.) | < 1 mA | Derived |

| **Calc 2a — Sensing PCB typical current (Wi-Fi/ESP-NOW radio idle)** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I1 = 23.88 mA   (ESP32-S3, active/idle) I2 = 8 mA       (MPU-6050 breakout) I3 = 0.5 mA     (LM358N, quiescent) I4 = 5 mA       (RBD-2229, estimated) I5 = 0.5 mA     (pull-ups / misc) |
| **Formula** | I_typ = I1 + I2 + I3 + I4 + I5 |
| **Substitution** | I_typ = 23.88 + 8 + 0.5 + 5 + 0.5   (mA) |
| **Result** | **I_typ = 37.88 mA  ≈  37.9 mA** |

| **Given** | V = 3.3 V   (LDO output rail) I_typ = 0.0379 A   (from Calc 2a) |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | P_typ = V x I_typ |
| **Substitution** | P_typ = 3.3 x 0.0379 |
| **Result** | **P_typ = 0.125 W** |

| **Calc 2b — Sensing PCB peak current (Wi-Fi/ESP-NOW TX burst)** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I1 = 355 mA   (ESP32-S3, TX peak, 802.11b worst case) I2 = 8 mA     (MPU-6050 breakout) I3 = 0.5 mA   (LM358N, quiescent) I4 = 5 mA     (RBD-2229, estimated) I5 = 0.5 mA   (pull-ups / misc) |
| **Formula** | I_pk = I1 + I2 + I3 + I4 + I5 |
| **Substitution** | I_pk = 355 + 8 + 0.5 + 5 + 0.5   (mA) |
| **Result** | **I_pk = 369 mA** |

| **Given** | V = 3.3 V   (LDO output rail) I_pk = 0.369 A   (from Calc 2b) |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | P_pk = V x I_pk |
| **Substitution** | P_pk = 3.3 x 0.369 |
| **Result** | **P_pk = 1.22 W   (momentary — packet-length duration only)** |

The AP2112K-3.3 is rated for 600 mA continuous [6]; the 369 mA peak sits at 61% of that rating. Because it's a linear regulator, input (battery) current tracks output current 1:1 — this is also the current the polyfuse and battery see.

# 3. Alarm PCB current & power budget

The ESP32-WROOM-32 runs from 3.3V (its own LDO); the RGB LED and buzzer are driven directly from BAT+ per the 3.6 schematic. Both contribute to what the polyfuse sees, since the 3.3V rail is itself derived from BAT+.

| **Component** | **Condition** | **Current** | **Source** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| ESP32-WROOM-32 | Active, RF idle (Stage-1 standby/indicator) | ≈ 30 – 40 mA | Order-of-magnitude, [11] |
| ESP32-WROOM-32 | Wi-Fi RX | 95 – 100 mA | LastMinuteEngineers [11] |
| ESP32-WROOM-32 | Wi-Fi TX (peak) | 180 – 240 mA | LastMinuteEngineers [11] |
| RGB LED (worst case, all 3 ch.) | 220 Ω, BAT+ 3.0–4.2V minus Vf | 2 – 10 mA/ch → ≤30 mA | Derived from schematic 3.6 |
| Passive piezo buzzer | PWM ~2–4 kHz via 100 Ω | ≈ 1 – 3 mA (assumed) | Unverified — see §8 |

| **Calc 3a — Alarm PCB typical current (standby, one LED channel, buzzer off)** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I1 = 35 mA   (ESP32-WROOM-32, active/RF idle) I2 = 3 mA    (RGB LED, one channel on) I3 = 0 mA    (buzzer off) |
| **Formula** | I_typ = I1 + I2 + I3 |
| **Substitution** | I_typ = 35 + 3 + 0   (mA) |
| **Result** | **I_typ = 38 mA** |

| **Calc 3b — Alarm PCB peak current (Wi-Fi TX + worst-case all-channel alarm + buzzer)** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I1 = 240 mA   (ESP32-WROOM-32, TX peak) I2 = 30 mA    (RGB LED, all 3 channels, worst case) I3 = 3 mA     (passive buzzer, estimated) |
| **Formula** | I_pk = I1 + I2 + I3 |
| **Substitution** | I_pk = 240 + 30 + 3   (mA) |
| **Result** | **I_pk = 273 mA** |

# 4. Overcurrent protection — polyfuse validation

4.1 Reference part

The schematic specifies a "500 mA resettable" through-hole polyfuse. The closest documented match is the Littelfuse RXEF050 radial PTC [7]:

| **Parameter** | **Symbol** | **Value** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hold current (max. sustained, no trip) | Ihold | 500 mA |
| Trip current (min. current that will trip it) | Itrip | 1 A |
| Max fault current (no damage) | Imax | 40 A |
| Time to trip (rated fault condition) | — | 4 s |
| Initial / post-trip resistance | Ri / R1 | 500 mΩ / 1.17 Ω |
| Max voltage rating | Vmax | 72 V |

4.2 Per-board margin (each board on its own fuse)

| **Calc 4a — Sensing PCB fuse margin** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I_pk = 369 mA   (Calc 2b) Ihold = 500 mA   (Ref. RXEF050) |
| **Formula** | Margin_hold = (Ihold - I_pk) / Ihold x 100% |
| **Substitution** | Margin_hold = (500 - 369) / 500 x 100% |
| **Result** | **Margin_hold = 26.2% below Ihold** |

| **Given** | I_pk = 369 mA   (Calc 2b) Itrip = 1000 mA   (Ref. RXEF050) |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | Margin_trip = Itrip / I_pk |
| **Substitution** | Margin_trip = 1000 / 369 |
| **Result** | **Margin_trip = 2.71x below Itrip** |

| **Calc 4b — Alarm PCB fuse margin** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I_pk = 273 mA   (Calc 3b) Ihold = 500 mA   (Ref. RXEF050) |
| **Formula** | Margin_hold = (Ihold - I_pk) / Ihold x 100% |
| **Substitution** | Margin_hold = (500 - 273) / 500 x 100% |
| **Result** | **Margin_hold = 45.4% below Ihold** |

| **Given** | I_pk = 273 mA   (Calc 3b) Itrip = 1000 mA   (Ref. RXEF050) |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | Margin_trip = Itrip / I_pk |
| **Substitution** | Margin_trip = 1000 / 273 |
| **Result** | **Margin_trip = 3.66x below Itrip** |

With a dedicated fuse per board, neither board's worst-case transient gets close to Ihold. The 500 mA part is correctly sized for this scenario.

4.3 Combined scenario (single shared battery + single shared fuse)

| **Calc 4c — Combined peak current** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I_sensing_pk = 369 mA   (Calc 2b) I_alarm_pk = 273 mA   (Calc 3b) |
| **Formula** | I_combined = I_sensing_pk + I_alarm_pk |
| **Substitution** | I_combined = 369 + 273 |
| **Result** | **I_combined = 642 mA** |

| **Given** | I_combined = 642 mA   (above) Ihold = 500 mA   (Ref. RXEF050) |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | Overshoot% = (I_combined - Ihold) / Ihold x 100% |
| **Substitution** | Overshoot% = (642 - 500) / 500 x 100% |
| **Result** | **Overshoot% = 28.4% ABOVE Ihold** |

| **A single shared 500 mA fuse is undersized for this combined case.** 642 mA exceeds the 500 mA hold current by 28.4%. Ihold is the maximum current the device tolerates indefinitely without tripping, so a sustained combined draw at this level will eventually trip the fuse, even though it never reaches the 1A trip-current spec. This only matters if both boards' Wi-Fi/ESP-NOW TX bursts overlap long enough to count as sustained rather than a brief joint transient. Fix if a shared supply is intended: keep separate fuses per board (simplest), or upsize the shared fuse to ≥750 mA hold current. |
| --- |

4.4 Battery discharge margin

| **Calc 4d — Battery C-rate under worst-case combined draw** |
| --- |
| **Given** | I_combined = 642 mA   (Calc 4c) Battery capacity = 1000 mAh |
| **Formula** | C-rate = I_combined / Capacity |
| **Substitution** | C-rate = 642 mA / 1000 mAh |
| **Result** | **C-rate = 0.64C** |

Most generic single-cell LiPo packs are rated for at least 1C continuous, so the cell is not the binding constraint in either scenario — the polyfuse is.

# 5. LDO headroom note (AP2112K-3.3)

The AP2112K-3.3 regulates correctly only while VBAT stays above VOUT plus its dropout voltage:

| **Calc 5 — Minimum battery voltage for regulation to hold** |
| --- |
| **Given** | VOUT = 3.3 V   (LDO setpoint) Vdropout = 0.25 V   (typical at 600 mA, Ref. [6]) |
| **Formula** | VBAT_min = VOUT + Vdropout |
| **Substitution** | VBAT_min = 3.3 + 0.25 |
| **Result** | **VBAT_min = 3.55 V** |

Below VBAT ≈ 3.55V — as the LiPo approaches its ~3.0V discharge floor — the LDO enters dropout and the 3.3V rail sags below spec. This is a firmware/brown-out consideration, not a fuse or wiring issue, but it's worth handling in software so the ESP32 shuts down cleanly rather than behaving unpredictably on a nearly-dead cell.

# 6. Simulation-verified headroom (Proteus export)

6.1 Op-amp output vs. supply rails

Pulled directly from the exported Proteus graph data (FILT OUT trace, 9-second run, 42,064 samples) — measured, not assumed:

| **Calc 6a — Peak-to-peak swing** |
| --- |
| **Given** | V_hi = 0.9347 V   (measured peak high, t = 1.639 s) V_lo = 0.8343 V   (measured peak low, t = 1.516 s) |
| **Formula** | Swing = V_hi - V_lo |
| **Substitution** | Swing = 0.9347 - 0.8343 |
| **Result** | **Swing = 0.100 V** |

| **Given** | Swing = 0.100 V   (above) Rail range = 3.3V - 0V = 3.3 V |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | %Used = Swing / Rail_range x 100% |
| **Substitution** | %Used = 0.1004 / 3.3 x 100% |
| **Result** | **%Used = 3.0%** |

| **Given** | V_lo = 0.8343 V   (measured peak low) V_hi = 0.9347 V   (measured peak high) Rails at 0 V and 3.3 V |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | Headroom = min( V_lo - 0V ,  3.3V - V_hi ) |
| **Substitution** | Headroom = min( 0.8343 ,  3.3 - 0.9347 ) = min( 0.834 ,  2.365 ) |
| **Result** | **Headroom = 0.834 V   (to the nearer rail, 0V)** |

The signal is nowhere near clipping — it uses only 3% of the available window. If anything, this suggests there's room to increase gain without any clipping risk, assuming this run represents realistic event amplitude (worth double-checking against the intended worst-case ObsPy input).

6.2 Piezo signal current — real data vs. generic assumption

Re-derived using the actual raw piezo-node signal (C1(1) trace) instead of a hypothetical sinusoid. The governing relationship for a capacitive source is I = C x dV/dt:

| **Calc 6b — Peak signal current from measured dV/dt** |
| --- |
| **Given** | dV/dt = 4.66 V/s   (peak observed in simulation, t = 1.500 s) C = 10 / 35 / 50 nF   (generic disc range, Ref. [12] — RBD-2229 itself unpublished) |
| **Formula** | I_peak = C x dV/dt |
| **Substitution** | I_peak(10nF) = 10e-9 x 4.66 = 4.66e-8 A I_peak(35nF) = 35e-9 x 4.66 = 1.63e-7 A I_peak(50nF) = 50e-9 x 4.66 = 2.33e-7 A |
| **Result** | **I_peak = 0.047 uA  /  0.163 uA  /  0.233 uA   (10 / 35 / 50 nF respectively)** |

| **Given** | dV/dt_p99 = 3.05 V/s   (99th-percentile, outlier-robust) C = 35 nF   (measured generic disc, Ref. [12]) |
| --- | --- |
| **Formula** | I_p99 = C x dV/dt_p99 |
| **Substitution** | I_p99 = 35e-9 x 3.05 |
| **Result** | **I_p99 = 0.107 uA   (robust check against the single-sample peak above)** |

Roughly four orders of magnitude below the milliamp-scale currents already in the budget (compare to Calc 2a/2b) — confirmed negligible, not just assumed. No revision to §2's power budget is needed.

# 7. Charging-path notes (TP4056, LM2596)

TP4056 standby current is ≈50-55 µA and shutdown current ≈40-55 µA — negligible against everything else in this report [9]. Charge current is set by RPROG; a 1.3 kΩ resistor (common on generic breakout boards) gives ≈1.1A typical, so confirm the actual RPROG value on your specific module before relying on that number.

| **TP4056 is not a bare USB-to-3.3V pass-through.** Its protection circuitry assumes a LiPo cell is present on OUT+. If "direct charger" mode is ever meant to mean running the boards from USB with no battery installed, that's outside the intended use of this IC — fine with the battery present, risky without it. |
| --- |

| **LM2596 cannot run from a single-cell LiPo.** The LM2596 needs roughly 4.5-5V in to regulate correctly [10]; a single-cell LiPo only ever supplies 3.0-4.2V. If it's wired to BAT+, it simply will not produce an output on battery power — only while USB is connected. This directly conflicts with the stated requirement to support both battery-only and direct-charger operation, and is the one item in this report that needs an actual design decision, not just a calculation. Resolution paths: feed the LM2596 from raw VBUS (5V) instead of BAT+ and accept it only works while plugged in; drop the buck converter from the battery-powered path entirely and let the LDO handle 3.3V as already validated in §2/§3; or clarify what the LM2596's actual intended role is so this section can be completed. |
| --- |

# 8. Figures not available from any datasheet

- RBD-2229 piezo module current draw — RoboticsBD's listing gives dimensions and I/O type but no supply-current spec. Estimated at ≈5 mA by analogy to comparator-equipped modules in the same catalog line.

- Passive piezo buzzer current draw — no datasheet was available for the specific part; the ≈1-3 mA estimate is an order-of-magnitude placeholder.

Both are small enough that even a 3-5× error would not change the fuse-margin conclusions in §4, but a bench measurement (multimeter in series with each module at 3.3V) is the only way to close this gap with certainty.

